	"title"			"Trader Ships (Continued)"
	"description"		"/ Trader Ships (Continued)\n\n AUTOMATIC1111 Trader ships 1.7 IoIqq \n\n 32 \n\n 9 \n\n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n\n 4~8 14~20 \n 10~18 \n\n 40 80 \n\n +100 6 \n\n 60% \n\n \n \n\n32 80 + 2\nTrade\n\n 1.7 1.1~1.6 \nHarmony \n\n Harmony \n\n DLC Royalty \n\n1.7 1.1~1.6\n Def Def \n\nAUTOMATIC1111 \nIoIqq 1.7 \n\nTrader Ships (Continued)\n\nTrade ships land on your map and do business with you face to face - no orbital trader, no trade beacon.\n\nThe RimWorld 1.7 continuation of AUTOMATIC1111's Trader ships, ported and extended by IoIqq.\n\nNew in this version\n\nTrade Desk\nA standalone powered 3x2 building with the same research as the comms console and a lower cost. Right-click it to call or buy ships without touching the console.\n\nFaction fleets\nEvery faction automatically gets up to 9 themed trade ships drawn from its own item pool:\n\nMedical - medicine and medical supplies\nWeapon - weapons and ammo, with some medicine\nApparel - clothing, armour, headgear\nFood - prepared meals and drinks\nSupply - metal, leather, textiles, minerals\nIndustrial - components, fuel, chemicals\nFurniture - furniture and building materials\nRelic - artifacts, relics, ultratech\nGeneral - a bit of everything\n\nPlus two ships that sell people:\n\nTalent - 4-8 people with skills 14-20 and a passion, who join as colonists\nSlave - 10-18 people, cheaper and in bulk, who arrive as slaves\n\nStock\nStock is drawn from vanilla categories and trade tags matching the theme: around 40 item kinds on a normal ship, around 80 on food and general ships, with no duplicate variants.\n\nMedical ships really only sell medicine; weapon ships carry no food. Every ship buys back the categories it deals in, and the \"will buy\" tab lists them in full.\n\nCharter and purchase\nPay silver to call a specific ship type - better goodwill means a lower fee (40% off at +100). You can also buy a shuttle of your own from a faction, priced by goodwill.\n\nShips can be attacked\nBelow 60% health the captain demands compensation. Three outcomes:\n\nPay up and it leaves\nRefuse - goodwill drops, and a letter tells you exactly how much\nRefuse and accept the consequences - the faction turns hostile\n\nDestroy the ship outright and the entire cargo spills onto the ground, while the faction becomes an enemy.\n\nThe captain only demands compensation when your own colonists or turrets did the damage. Fires, lightning and other factions' fights are not charged to you.\n\nStowaways\nA ship may drop off a factionless person who wanders near your colony. Take them in and they join; turn them away and they leave.\n\nHow to use\n\nResearch Microelectronics, then find Trade Desk in the architect menu (3x2, 80 steel + 2 components, needs power)\nRight-click the desk with a colonist and pick Trade\nPick a faction on the left, a ship type on the right, then hit Call\nOnce it lands, walk a colonist over and trade\n\nLanding zones\n\nTwo tools live in the Zone category: Expand landing zone and Clear landing zone.\n\nShips aim for the centre of the whole zone - if you want several ships parked side by side, paint several separate small zones rather than one big one.\n\nRequirements\n\nRimWorld 1.7 (older 1.1-1.6 folders are still bundled)\nHarmony - optional\n\nWhat happens without Harmony? Ships still arrive and still trade; core functionality is unaffected. Only one thing is lost: the comms console no longer gains a \"Trade desk...\" submenu - use the standalone building instead and everything else is identical.\n\nNo DLC required. With Royalty, ships will also use your ship landing beacons as a fallback landing spot.\n\nCompatibility\n\nVersion: 1.7 (1.1-1.6 also declared)\nAdds new Defs (trade desk, generated ship types); does not rewrite vanilla Def values\nSafe to add to an existing save\nRemoving it mid-save is not recommended - ships currently trading will be lost, and the landing zone is dropped too\nHeavy UI mods that rework the trade dialog may cause the ship list to misalign\n\nWhy bother\n\nVanilla orbital traders are a phone call and a menu. This mod makes ships actually land on your map. You have to walk a pawn out to them, the cargo is physically on board, and a ship you damage will spill its goods, demand compensation, or turn on you. Trading finally has a physical presence.\n\nCredits\n\nAUTOMATIC1111 - original mod: design, code, ship art.\nIoIqq - 1.7 port and the additions above.\n\nIf you are the original author and would prefer this not exist, contact me and I will take it down."
	"changenote"		"1.8.2 - Rebuilt the mod against the current RimWorld 1.7 with no gameplay changes. Fixes the trade desk not showing up in the architect menu and a load-time error in developer mode. Should work on existing saves as before."
}
